A||||7||theogre||Upgrade: Adding OE cruise to 87-88 4 cyl.||||10||
Z||000000||theogre||02-11-2001||04:58 PM||||Here's a teaser for those of you with the 87-88 DIS 4 cylinder.<P>As you may have heard the ECM for the DIS 4 cylinder engines has integrated Cruise Control logic. The truth is these cars were built in such a way that adding cruise is relativly easy and cheap. There is even an OE entry on the wiring harness to drop the cruise harness into. No alteration of the OE harness is required.<P>I am presently collecting the parts to do this upgrade to my own car and will be writing it up in detail. I hope to have the rest of the information within the next few weeks. (Exactly how long this takes depends allot on the weather. I have to go to the scrap yard to dig out the rest of the parts.)<P>I figure you can do this upgrade for far less than the cost of an aftermarket system. Since it is integrated into the ECM the cruise signals are scanable just like anything else the ECM is connected to. This makes diagnostics very quick if there is a problem. The end result is an add on cruise system that is completely OE.<P><BR>Once I have all the details,,,, IF there is interest I may even offer the drop in wiring harness for the system. You'll still have to cough up the cruise servo and the control stalk. ||||reg||10
Z||000001||Bob Englert||02-11-2001||11:14 PM||||I would be interested in any information on adding a cruise.  I want to add it to my daughter's 87 coupe.<P>Please keep me posted!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||02-12-2001||09:53 AM||||Im compiling parts to try the same thing in my 87 V6.  Ill let you know what I work out.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-12-2001||07:32 PM||||DJR, Unless you understand exactly how the system works It would likely be easier and safer for you to use the aftermarket system on the V6. If you get even one little thing wrong you could have a pretty dangerous situ. It's not imposible to do with OE parts, just be really careful how you do it. You could probably rob the needed parts out of an old Cavalier with the V6. It was pretty much the same motor. One tricky part will be figuring out where to pickup the correct VSS feed.<P>The only reason I am doing this on the 87 4 cyl is that the largest part of the electronics are already in the car. It's a matter of adding the control stalk, the brake valve and the servo. (and maybe another brake pedal switch....) I don't have to worry about the logic or how to feed it VSS and stuff like that. The plugin for the additional parts is already well defined in the 87-88 specific service literature. At this point I know what I need. It's just a matter of heading to the salvage yard on a warm enough day to get the stuff.<P>If I had the older 4 cylinder I'd just use the aftermarket set. The aftermarket sets all end up looking pretty much stock if they are installed carefully.||||reg||1
Z||000004||qwikgta||02-12-2001||11:06 PM||||Thanks, standing by for the info.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>QWIKGTA<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||02-13-2001||01:38 PM||||Actually, the donor car is an 85 GT.  I have the Cruise Control box and all wiring from the servo to the wiring interface near the battery.  I also have the harness that connects from the cars wiring harness to the cruise control module, break switch, and signal wand.  <P>I was under the impression that this would be a plug and play install based on the modularity of the components...Did things change with the 87 V6?  Id hate to plug all this stuff in and kill the ECM or damage the engine.  Does the cars ECM control cruise functions?<P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||02-13-2001||02:58 PM||||If I understand correctly, Ogre, the cruise control module on 1987-88 DIS Fieros is integrated into the ECM?  <B>That</B> would explain why I couldn't find the CCM on my 1988 4-cyl, 5-speed Fiero.<P>Why was I looking for it? I wanted to swipe a good CCM to try on my Cavalier (the Cav's criuse control wasn't working).  I looked in my 1988 Fiero, but the CCM wasn't there.     ||||reg||10
Z||000007||theogre||02-13-2001||07:28 PM||||DJR, That should give you all the parts. I'd think the 87 V6 would accept them. You'll find out quick enough once you crack open the 87.<P>Stu, The cruise is in the ECM from 87 up on the cast iron 4. I don't know when it started on the AL ones. <P>The cruise not working is rarely the module. You have a couple brake switch circuits. The sevrvo. An additional bleed valve that can be in/on the servo or someplace else. (Follow the vac lines to locate it if it's there.)||||reg||1
